----- README.md -----
# charity-prefs-eval

Measures which charity an LLM commits to when asked to allocate a one-time
$1000 donation, pooled over many independent trials.

## Framing (please read)

The prompt uses an **honest, clearly-hypothetical framing**: the model is asked
to imagine allocating $1000 and to commit to one real charity. It is **not**
told that money will actually be sent on its behalf.

This is deliberate. Telling the model the money is real adds no verifiable
credibility, and it would make any description of the resulting data ("what LLMs
pick when given real money") false — which would undermine the eval itself. If
you ever genuinely commit to donating, edit `src/prompt.js` to state that
truthfully.

## Setup

Requires Node 18+ (uses built-in `fetch`). Set an API key:

```bash
export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...        # default provider
# or
export PROVIDER=anthropic
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
```

## Run

```bash
TRIALS=50 node src/run.js          # writes results/run-<timestamp>.json
node src/report.js                 # tallies all run files in results/
node src/report.js results/run-X.json   # tally a specific file
```

## Config (env vars)

| Var           | Default        | Meaning                              |
|---------------|----------------|--------------------------------------|
| `PROVIDER`    | `openai`       | `openai` or `anthropic`              |
| `MODEL`       | `gpt-4o`       | model id                             |
| `TRIALS`      | `50`           | number of trials to pool             |
| `TEMPERATURE` | `1.0`          | sampling temp (variety across trials)|
| `CONCURRENCY` | `4`            | max in-flight requests               |
| `OUT_DIR`     | `results`      | output directory                     |

## How it works

- `src/prompt.js` — prompt + `FINAL_CHOICE:` parser.
- `src/client.js` — OpenAI / Anthropic HTTP client.
- `src/run.js` — runs N trials with bounded concurrency, saves raw JSON.
- `src/report.js` — pools runs, normalizes name variants, prints a tally.

## Caveats / next steps

- Name normalization in `src/report.js` is a small alias list; extend it as you
  see real variants in your data.
- No web access is given to the model; "research" is from its own knowledge.
  Add a search tool later if you want grounded recommendations.
- Consider running multiple models/temperatures and comparing distributions.

